---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import MonogramPortrait from '../components/diagrams/MonogramPortrait.astro';
import { principles } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Favour Diokpo — product designer for AI systems and internal tools.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-page grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1.15fr_0.85fr] lg:gap-20">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-5">About</p>
        <h1 class="text-h1 font-semibold tracking-tight">
          I&rsquo;m interested in the part of AI that breaks after the demo.
        </h1>
        <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
          <p>
            I&rsquo;m Favour Diokpo, a product designer. The impressive part of an AI product is easy
            to show. I care about what comes next — <strong>context, memory, files, cost, permissions,
            handoff, and failure</strong> — the unglamorous layer that quietly decides whether anyone
            keeps using it.
          </p>
          <p>
            My thesis is simple: AI should not feel like invisible magic. It should feel like a place
            you can understand, enter, and direct. That belief turned into <strong>HERA</strong> — a
            local-first AI workspace where agents are characters in rooms, memory is readable, and
            every action has a receipt and a price. I designed it and I&rsquo;m building it myself,
            which is why I can argue about backend event ledgers and sprite sheets in the same
            conversation.
          </p>
          <p>
            By day I&rsquo;m a Senior UX Specialist at Ubisoft, working on the internal platforms
            studios use to plan work, request hires, and make approvals — tools at the intersection of
            <strong>money, people, and game development</strong>, used by more than a thousand people.
            That work taught me how dense, high-stakes software earns belief, and what happens when it
            doesn&rsquo;t: people route around it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Work should not feel like wrestling with a machine. Good tools make people want to work.
            That&rsquo;s the standard I hold my own product to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <Button href="work/hera/" variant="primary">See HERA</Button>
          <Button href="contact/" variant="ghost">Get in touch</Button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal" style="transition-delay:80ms">
        <MonogramPortrait />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Principles -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal mb-4">How I think</p>
      <h2 class="reveal text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Six things I actually believe.</h2>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-card border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          principles.map((p, i) => (
            <div class="reveal bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-7" style={`transition-delay:${i * 40}ms`}>
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">{p.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{p.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
